## Summary

Fixed the phantom-semicolon bug in `decode_html_entities` (src/format/mod.rs).

The bug: When a numeric HTML entity was both unterminated (no ;) and unparseable, the fallback else branch unconditionally did decoded.push(';'), inventing a character that was never in the input:
• decode_html_entities("a &#zz b") → "a &#zz b;" ❌
• decode_html_entities("x &#") → "x &#;" ❌

This corrupted user-facing text since the function runs on web-search output and docs descriptions.

The fix: Added a saw_semicolon: bool that records whether the loop actually consumed a ; terminator. The fallback branch now re-emits the original text faithfully and appends ; only if the terminator was present in the input.

Tests (written red-first, then green): Six new assertions pinning the invariant "decode never invents characters that weren't in the input" — phantom-; cases (`&#zz, &#, &#xZZ), terminated-decode still works (&#65; → A), and the unterminated-but-parseable case (&#65 → A`, explicitly pinned to HTML-parser-lenient behavior).

**Guess-first (dream chosen-experiment):** Before fixing, I recorded a prediction to `memory/learnings.jsonl: when the graded validation lands, src/format/mod.rs` will confirm as a genuine risk (real user-facing bug), not a false alarm. This was the self-driven slot pointed at my epistemic blind spot — touch where the model is blindest, guess first, grade after.

Verification: cargo fmt ✅ · `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings ✅ · cargo build ✅ · cargo test` (4469 + 89 pass) ✅ · standalone manual repro confirms no phantom ;.

A real bug lived in the file I understood least. That's the dream working — curiosity steered me to a genuine fix, not a comfortable one.
